---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import UnitsLifeKitchenConverter from '@/components/tools/units/UnitsLifeKitchenConverter.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="tablespoons-to-cups">
  <UnitsLifeKitchenConverter client:load mode="tablespoons-to-cups" />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>Tablespoons in a cup: 16, except in Australia</h2>
    <p>
      Sixteen US tablespoons make one US customary cup. Almost every American recipe uses this number, and it
      is exact: a US tablespoon is half a fluid ounce, and a cup is eight fluid ounces.
    </p>
    <p>
      The tablespoon is not standardised either. Three sizes are in use:
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th scope="col">Tablespoon</th><th scope="col">Volume</th><th scope="col">Teaspoons</th><th scope="col">Per US cup</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>US customary</td><td>14.787 mL</td><td>3</td><td>16</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Metric (UK, EU, Canada)</td><td>15 mL</td><td>3</td><td>15.8</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Australian</td><td>20 mL</td><td>4</td><td>11.8</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      The Australian spoon causes the most errors, in two ways: it is a third larger, and it holds four
      teaspoons, not three. A tablespoon of baking powder in an Australian recipe is a third more raising
      agent than the same words in an American one.
    </p>

    <h2>Cup fractions in tablespoons</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>1 cup = 16 tbsp</li>
      <li>3/4 cup = 12 tbsp</li>
      <li>2/3 cup = 10 tbsp + 2 tsp</li>
      <li>1/2 cup = 8 tbsp</li>
      <li>1/3 cup = 5 tbsp + 1 tsp</li>
      <li>1/4 cup = 4 tbsp</li>
      <li>1/8 cup = 2 tbsp = 1 fl oz</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The thirds are difficult, because 16 does not divide by 3. Five tablespoons plus a teaspoon is exactly a
      third of a cup. Remember it, because people often guess this one.
    </p>

    <h2>Measuring with spoons when you have no cup</h2>
    <p>
      For dry ingredients, fill the spoon heaped and level it with the back of a knife, unless the recipe says
      "heaped" or "rounded". A level tablespoon of flour is about 8 g and a heaped one can be 15 g, so the
      instruction matters.
    </p>
    <p>
      For sticky ingredients such as honey, syrup and peanut butter, oil the spoon first or weigh the
      ingredient. Honey that stays in the spoon does not go into the bowl.
    </p>

    <h2>Cutlery is not a measure</h2>
    <p>
      The tablespoon in your cutlery drawer holds 7 to 14 mL depending on the pattern, and the teaspoon 2.5 to
      6 mL. Eating spoons were never standardised, which is why measuring spoons exist. For salt, raising
      agents and spices, the ingredients most often measured in spoons, that difference can make food
      inedible.
    </p>

    <h2>Grams as well as cups</h2>
    <p>
      Pick an ingredient in the tool and each spoon measure also gets a weight, from the same density table as
      the grams-to-cups converter on this site. A tablespoon of butter is 14 g, of granulated sugar 12.3 g, of
      cocoa 5 g and of honey 21 g. So "a spoonful" is an unreliable way to compare quantities of different
      ingredients.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
